{
235: {skill_tested: "Identify why material sorting added value.", question: "According to the passage, why did dealers sort cloth into grades?", option1: "To help mills estimate the fiber quality and processing needs of each load", option2: "To ensure that every paper product used white linen", option3: "To conceal buttons and seams inside the bales", option4: "To prevent households from saving textile scraps", correctAnswer: "To help mills estimate the fiber quality and processing needs of each load", explanation: "Step 1: Different fibers, colors, and attachments required different processing and produced different paper.<br><br>Step 2: Grades turned a mixed load into categories whose likely yield mills could estimate.<br><br>Answer: To help mills estimate the fiber quality and processing needs of each load", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
236: {skill_tested: "Identify a cause of chronic supply pressure.", question: "What structural problem caused rag supply to lag behind growing paper demand?", option1: "Collectors refused to transport cloth near mills.", option2: "Textile rags became available only as existing cloth wore out.", option3: "Governments required all rags to be exported.", option4: "Wood pulp had already eliminated printing.", correctAnswer: "Textile rags became available only as existing cloth wore out.", explanation: "Step 1: Paper use expanded rapidly.<br><br>Step 2: Rag production depended on the slower rate at which households wore out textiles.<br><br>Answer: Textile rags became available only as existing cloth wore out.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
237: {skill_tested: "Identify the central historical interpretation.", question: "Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", option1: "Wood pulp removed every hidden cost from papermaking.", option2: "Rag dealers disappeared immediately after the first wood-pulp experiment.", option3: "The shift from rags to wood reorganized papermaking's supply, labor, and risks rather than simply substituting a better ingredient.", option4: "Sorting cloth required no specialized knowledge.", correctAnswer: "The shift from rags to wood reorganized papermaking's supply, labor, and risks rather than simply substituting a better ingredient.", explanation: "Step 1: The rag system coordinated collection and sorting but faced scarcity and labor risks.<br><br>Step 2: Wood enabled expansion while creating new needs for land, machines, chemicals, and capital.<br><br>Answer: The shift from rags to wood reorganized papermaking's supply, labor, and risks rather than simply substituting a better ingredient.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
238: {skill_tested: "Analyze how market incentives produced harmful behavior.", question: "The theft and artificially dampened bundles are included primarily to show that:", option1: "wet cloth always produced stronger paper.", option2: "export restrictions immediately solved the supply shortage.", option3: "publishers preferred stolen textiles.", option4: "high prices during scarcity encouraged attempts to capture value dishonestly.", correctAnswer: "high prices during scarcity encouraged attempts to capture value dishonestly.", explanation: "Step 1: Paper demand pushed rag prices upward when supply was tight.<br><br>Step 2: Theft obtained salable cloth, while water fraudulently increased weight.<br><br>Step 3: Both behaviors are responses to the increased value of scarce material.<br><br>Answer: high prices during scarcity encouraged attempts to capture value dishonestly.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
239: {skill_tested: "Infer why wood pulp supported greater expansion.", question: "Wood pulp reduced mills' dependence on rag markets mainly because wood:", option1: "could be produced as a planned industrial input rather than only recovered after another product's use.", option2: "required no machinery or chemical treatment.", option3: "created paper identical to every rag-based paper.", option4: "was sorted entirely by households.", correctAnswer: "could be produced as a planned industrial input rather than only recovered after another product's use.", explanation: "Step 1: Rag availability depended on discarded textiles.<br><br>Step 2: Trees could be harvested specifically to supply papermaking.<br><br>Step 3: Mills could expand a dedicated input chain more directly than household wear-out.<br><br>Answer: could be produced as a planned industrial input rather than only recovered after another product's use.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
240: {skill_tested: "Analyze continuity within industrial change.", question: "The passage mentions later recycling businesses primarily to emphasize that:", option1: "all rag warehouses became paper mills.", option2: "classification skills from the rag trade remained relevant even as materials and markets changed.", option3: "wood pulp made material separation unnecessary.", option4: "textile collectors controlled every later recycling industry.", correctAnswer: "classification skills from the rag trade remained relevant even as materials and markets changed.", explanation: "Step 1: Dealers adapted or moved into other reusable materials.<br><br>Step 2: Later recycling also depends on separating materials by composition.<br><br>Step 3: An operational principle survived beyond the original commodity.<br><br>Answer: classification skills from the rag trade remained relevant even as materials and markets changed.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
241: {skill_tested: "Interpret the distribution of industrial risk.", question: "The statement that the rag system transferred risk downward means that:", option1: "paper mills performed every hazardous sorting task themselves.", option2: "households set stable prices for all collectors.", option3: "less powerful participants bore low returns, unstable prices, and dangerous work within a profitable supply chain.", option4: "publishers accepted all losses caused by contaminated cloth.", correctAnswer: "less powerful participants bore low returns, unstable prices, and dangerous work within a profitable supply chain.", explanation: "Step 1: Households received little, collectors faced volatile prices, and sorters faced physical hazards.<br><br>Step 2: Their work enabled the industrial paper supply.<br><br>Step 3: Costs and uncertainty were concentrated among participants with less control over the market.<br><br>Answer: less powerful participants bore low returns, unstable prices, and dangerous work within a profitable supply chain.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
242: {skill_tested: "Evaluate a simplified historical account.", question: "Why does the author reject the claim that wood pulp merely replaced an inferior ingredient?", option1: "It incorrectly assumes that paper demand ever increased.", option2: "It gives too much attention to sorters' recognized public status.", option3: "It proves that wood pulp was never used on a large scale.", option4: "It overlooks both the complex coordination achieved by the rag system and the new dependencies created by wood production.", correctAnswer: "It overlooks both the complex coordination achieved by the rag system and the new dependencies created by wood production.", explanation: "Step 1: Rag supply organized saving, grading, transport, and skilled labor across many participants.<br><br>Step 2: Wood expansion relied on forests, machinery, chemicals, and capital.<br><br>Step 3: Calling one material simply superior hides a reorganization of systems, people, and costs.<br><br>Answer: It overlooks both the complex coordination achieved by the rag system and the new dependencies created by wood production.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
243: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the passage's concept of hidden dependence.", question: "The final comparison between rags and pulp most strongly suggests that a finished sheet of paper:", option1: "conceals the distinct resource and labor networks required to make it.", option2: "reveals the exact price paid to every worker in its supply chain.", option3: "has no relationship to landscapes or discarded materials.", option4: "can be used to identify its fiber source by sight alone.", correctAnswer: "conceals the distinct resource and labor networks required to make it.", explanation: "Step 1: Rag paper depended on textile reuse and distributed collection.<br><br>Step 2: Pulp paper depended on forests and capital-intensive processing.<br><br>Step 3: Readers see the page but ordinarily do not see either network or its allocated costs.<br><br>Answer: conceals the distinct resource and labor networks required to make it.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
